## testsmells.org 19-Smell Academic Catalog Audit

**Target:** `OrderService.Tests/OrderProcessorTests.cs` (MSTest 4.1, .NET 10)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 3           | 3              |
| Low      | 1           | 8 (all)        |
| **Total**| **8**       | **8 unique**   |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH SEVERITY

---

**1. Conditional Test Logic** — `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 24)

```csharp
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

**Risk:** One branch is never exercised — you don't know which assertion runs, hiding a gap.

**Fix:** Split into two deterministic tests with known inputs:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_OverThreshold_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 5) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_UnderThreshold_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-CHEAP", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

---

**2. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)** — `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)

```csharp
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
// ← no assertion, no expected-exception attribute
```

**Risk:** This always passes — even if `ProcessOrder` returns garbage or corrupts state. Provides false coverage confidence.

**Fix:** Assert on expected side-effects or return value:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_SingleItem_ReturnsProcessedResult()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
    Assert.IsTrue(_db.WasOrderSaved(order.Id));
}
```

---

**3. Sleepy Test** — `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 91)

```csharp
processor.ProcessOrderAsync(order);
Thread.Sleep(2000);
Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
```

**Risk:** Non-deterministic (races on slow CI), adds 2 s to every run. Will flake intermittently.

**Fix:** Use async/await with a deterministic signal or polling helper:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    await processor.ProcessOrderAsync(order);

    Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
}
